      en: "Salar de Tara is a large salt flat in the Atacama Desert of northern Chile, in the Antofagasta Region, at an elevation of about 4,200 meters. It is part of the Los Flamencos National Reserve and is known for its stark beauty, with white salt crusts, turquoise lagoons, and towering volcanoes. The area receives less than 200 mm of precipitation annually and has high solar radiation. It is an important habitat for flamingos, including the Chilean, Andean, and James's flamingo. The brine is rich in lithium and boron. The region contains ancient geoglyphs and archaeological sites from the Atacameño culture. The high altitude causes large diurnal temperature swings, from warm days to freezing nights.",
