      en: "Tupungato Volcano is a prominent stratovolcano in the Andes, on the Chile-Argentina border, in the Santiago Metropolitan Region. Rising to 6,570 meters, it is one of the tallest active volcanoes on Earth. It lies about 80 km east of Santiago and is part of the Cordillera Principal. The volcano has a symmetrical cone with a summit crater. It is covered by glaciers, which are retreating due to climate change. Tupungato is a popular but technically challenging climb. Its last eruption was in 1987, producing ash and lava. The region experiences frequent seismic activity. The volcano's name comes from the Mapuche language, meaning 'bright or shining'.",
